[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-138944"] :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t(16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-139063/selection/25fc09e0997e24130512ba4888642c984c51ff5e66d1f4e5464915b8d7e59fb7" :answer/id #uuid "f2c71fef-2c53-51bb-ac75-751ad118740c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139063/selection/6dea05a54454fee3d7074e0a3d2caf6cab1881ab10616ae9a8e5b55e66b230b5" :answer/id #uuid "179cbaec-1808-52d3-9809-5d4404ccdea8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139063/selection/89a8faf883bd3032ece69eb41113bac48ed6093dd0c8439b3c3076ed958b4e2a" :answer/id #uuid "5f8f5038-40f4-56d5-bb0c-8bcce5e7cebd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139063/selection/68506189bd9ad3ad48b7f5ca7ac6eebcc57480533bc7fdff9b541fae30d026ed" :answer/id #uuid "78d9868b-4d84-56a7-8111-111d44c4dcdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139063/selection/b05269135cef515e4e84870e5252d71451d91c7bf40f5cfc3592ff8ff3322957" :answer/id #uuid "71557663-b917-53e0-b083-f6fea59f4123" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139063/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "28f4c357-0ecd-5e3b-bee9-b8f2a3a3becc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139063/selection/25fc09e0997e24130512ba4888642c984c51ff5e66d1f4e5464915b8d7e59fb7" :answer-field/choices ["q-139063/selection/25fc09e0997e24130512ba4888642c984c51ff5e66d1f4e5464915b8d7e59fb7" "q-139063/selection/6dea05a54454fee3d7074e0a3d2caf6cab1881ab10616ae9a8e5b55e66b230b5" "q-139063/selection/89a8faf883bd3032ece69eb41113bac48ed6093dd0c8439b3c3076ed958b4e2a" "q-139063/selection/68506189bd9ad3ad48b7f5ca7ac6eebcc57480533bc7fdff9b541fae30d026ed" "q-139063/selection/b05269135cef515e4e84870e5252d71451d91c7bf40f5cfc3592ff8ff3322957"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139063" :question/id #uuid "a0f9299e-f9d5-5acc-90f0-a2ecc3cac381" :question/math-academy-id "q-139063" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139063/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139063"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139067/selection/ccf6d3f17a5b2346fda6628dc1b42b4207ca04d4dec622f7ffbb29a9eea0a58c" :answer/id #uuid "11590cd8-bd83-5d9e-926e-ce886c30e870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}(5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139067/selection/a910a3060668bb04687783f1e8d527eb9fa6b9565a60ae5f6da8e6252080a8a5" :answer/id #uuid "ceffd8b7-f5ec-5b6f-8c1f-f16a6fd5d828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139067/selection/141a8087d8e84d8658bf0657739246bcb584e30ec895cfc6fe32233d5c249c90" :answer/id #uuid "b8506327-466d-5536-8bb0-609a492d7b09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{5+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139067/selection/373803f9182868d6770891ba7bcc784e2c551257b4e1dc0e5a02896879c0a556" :answer/id #uuid "b1ba5c28-afe9-5e12-8aa1-0efe8ffbadce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5t+1)^{2}\\sqrt{5+2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139067/selection/5a0ea210e8102cd0084249c1479a63cfe34d8163095933b21766b5157bdc8726" :answer/id #uuid "751874ad-3297-5623-bdf5-417412c847b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139067/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "42109a03-8667-5b40-bc80-61b005d9935c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139067/selection/a910a3060668bb04687783f1e8d527eb9fa6b9565a60ae5f6da8e6252080a8a5" :answer-field/choices ["q-139067/selection/ccf6d3f17a5b2346fda6628dc1b42b4207ca04d4dec622f7ffbb29a9eea0a58c" "q-139067/selection/a910a3060668bb04687783f1e8d527eb9fa6b9565a60ae5f6da8e6252080a8a5" "q-139067/selection/141a8087d8e84d8658bf0657739246bcb584e30ec895cfc6fe32233d5c249c90" "q-139067/selection/373803f9182868d6770891ba7bcc784e2c551257b4e1dc0e5a02896879c0a556" "q-139067/selection/5a0ea210e8102cd0084249c1479a63cfe34d8163095933b21766b5157bdc8726"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139067" :question/id #uuid "0d9271f7-51c3-50a5-a620-a2ed152f61bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-139067" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5t+1,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+1)=5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(5)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139067/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139067"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-139069"] :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}4t\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-138968/selection/429275a5ff2346a993e63cc1aea0d4ab25164f01b6127f24c5bab1423c0d933d" :answer/id #uuid "b409fe45-0863-53d3-8e7a-d50040564ffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138968/selection/805887a0866385461955a44ecd9cb4d84c01a1c8d32dd68aebab7e81c8d6b253" :answer/id #uuid "c003096b-d9a1-5620-ad4d-b1e905582735" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138968/selection/23661ede757798ffc525445b87fc223468412ecaa49c95910af4e63603a171da" :answer/id #uuid "7054d531-a1c7-5357-818b-2ca133947d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138968/selection/3bea9d8cd4fc3892626f9d7d9c0a758fcdef06531ed423bbff4983f4a738d7c5" :answer/id #uuid "e11dedd0-66e8-5820-bfc0-6b06ce8fd018" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138968/selection/b600077a2351dac33f695b94456b8472d5548bb1fd4c81ce5fb874e580e7c308" :answer/id #uuid "8006a2f3-d72b-57ff-ac49-0b00fab6ba2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{3{t}^{3}+2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138968/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c36536-4311-5cd1-91df-15739c861c0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-138968/selection/3bea9d8cd4fc3892626f9d7d9c0a758fcdef06531ed423bbff4983f4a738d7c5" :answer-field/choices ["q-138968/selection/429275a5ff2346a993e63cc1aea0d4ab25164f01b6127f24c5bab1423c0d933d" "q-138968/selection/805887a0866385461955a44ecd9cb4d84c01a1c8d32dd68aebab7e81c8d6b253" "q-138968/selection/23661ede757798ffc525445b87fc223468412ecaa49c95910af4e63603a171da" "q-138968/selection/3bea9d8cd4fc3892626f9d7d9c0a758fcdef06531ed423bbff4983f4a738d7c5" "q-138968/selection/b600077a2351dac33f695b94456b8472d5548bb1fd4c81ce5fb874e580e7c308"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-138968" :question/id #uuid "df8c2841-327a-590b-8a05-04a1258f28e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138968" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}(4+9{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(4+9{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot |t|\\cdot \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot t\\cdot \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-138968/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-138968"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139027/selection/e36673fe9ec671158122b9216cd69b9d1f52b8f1a038e07099d175c12de7dd7e" :answer/id #uuid "b520d188-0200-5263-938f-8bd89a71dce0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t{cosh}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139027/selection/c47be02e76ac7e6d72475d4a1f420b46eff40272e415654ef4ba47d7b2dfec3e" :answer/id #uuid "c24d5099-f327-512b-a132-26a767949e32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{sinh}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{1+8{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139027/selection/ea6049abc2b48c33d449955012b4d768fa709cbaf433d268619165952992c185" :answer/id #uuid "6de76898-0b0a-5029-916b-b79a004a19ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139027/selection/abdaf7e9a56bb55d1c99fd8368a660a2e8239f7f4e09cf91ab2110b0a7716338" :answer/id #uuid "9357680e-316e-57b7-9575-f0b296cdd025" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139027/selection/b218c87b2aefdefeadae2fd5d492aa9a42929941f81e1be9efe514da5c4c5f61" :answer/id #uuid "e76fa795-791f-5ad9-bfdb-69a1760fd829" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t\\sqrt{1+4{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139027/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "201abff7-e9a2-5005-9f70-1fcb774d0319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139027/selection/ea6049abc2b48c33d449955012b4d768fa709cbaf433d268619165952992c185" :answer-field/choices ["q-139027/selection/e36673fe9ec671158122b9216cd69b9d1f52b8f1a038e07099d175c12de7dd7e" "q-139027/selection/c47be02e76ac7e6d72475d4a1f420b46eff40272e415654ef4ba47d7b2dfec3e" "q-139027/selection/ea6049abc2b48c33d449955012b4d768fa709cbaf433d268619165952992c185" "q-139027/selection/abdaf7e9a56bb55d1c99fd8368a660a2e8239f7f4e09cf91ab2110b0a7716338" "q-139027/selection/b218c87b2aefdefeadae2fd5d492aa9a42929941f81e1be9efe514da5c4c5f61"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139027" :question/id #uuid "b283cefd-2d92-55a0-bc22-17b538bd0f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-139027" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t)=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4{sinh}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4{sinh}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+16{sinh}^{2}⁡t{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t(1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139027/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139027"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-138981"] :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-139056/selection/e3f34b68694233af22d2d1a904d414b4c056195d8f8de4a528a0108fefbbbee2" :answer/id #uuid "d5b2aaa1-e602-582b-bed4-3ad5d78878ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139056/selection/117cae18577301091e022eefe46a462fb0b82a6cce93f73e53fd08b267e7e351" :answer/id #uuid "da125b7c-2ae1-5c74-94c7-ed3b9bd61f16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139056/selection/51121630aaf9a1c52f70aff21dcc71bb8968009a01cdce94678d51e9bdfdecef" :answer/id #uuid "ffb06bcc-49d8-5f94-96a9-2a82e56ec262" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139056/selection/042b084fe475a1d798982d5a45b6000a35d229d00c8b6d085e8d91c75ef01117" :answer/id #uuid "4ba90da3-8291-53c4-9945-267dc62d322b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139056/selection/1951223323d7d2f84afc853e64b63ae6802669e850bbb794c6bf99e36403ad48" :answer/id #uuid "a766c5a9-06e2-53e7-8030-fd3ffcbba21b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139056/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb923781-2af9-5032-bd24-51e51071c745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139056/selection/1951223323d7d2f84afc853e64b63ae6802669e850bbb794c6bf99e36403ad48" :answer-field/choices ["q-139056/selection/e3f34b68694233af22d2d1a904d414b4c056195d8f8de4a528a0108fefbbbee2" "q-139056/selection/117cae18577301091e022eefe46a462fb0b82a6cce93f73e53fd08b267e7e351" "q-139056/selection/51121630aaf9a1c52f70aff21dcc71bb8968009a01cdce94678d51e9bdfdecef" "q-139056/selection/042b084fe475a1d798982d5a45b6000a35d229d00c8b6d085e8d91c75ef01117" "q-139056/selection/1951223323d7d2f84afc853e64b63ae6802669e850bbb794c6bf99e36403ad48"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139056" :question/id #uuid "9be7a0b9-d57a-5f3e-8e9f-3d0abd1f1ee8" :question/math-academy-id "q-139056" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3},\\,y=2t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139056/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139056"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139033/selection/0da528af2c2acad7fec81ac76c9e2234623a78033071f03cfd0f0ba2dfe62328" :answer/id #uuid "ce1b6af5-68dc-5377-b228-1963e48de564" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139033/selection/f324691e69eb403cb0b9bdd1ac58cbe4b22fc42bac4b770eff8a1ad615df91f2" :answer/id #uuid "0f0c7492-e87a-5895-b079-779775aec485" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139033/selection/6d7038b0195af33f0b9d34b41262abc27fdb6a77628d13b17b3a8eee950a47b9" :answer/id #uuid "a4ebc455-c21c-5c5d-b22e-0e2681b67ee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139033/selection/bbbbd34f56a2efc420f6cbfa1a9dec7d83a77c89f28fb2022e019d544a5793ce" :answer/id #uuid "b60f39c8-31c3-5e73-9b4c-13c4977177b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139033/selection/d388a7a600d680356d80ea3c3d43fb27b9a478fb689c6394d4e57ee2f16559ee" :answer/id #uuid "1a7fc6da-1117-5e56-ab97-a60e92b21045" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139033/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc5f0aed-25df-58bf-becc-f80b60821016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139033/selection/d388a7a600d680356d80ea3c3d43fb27b9a478fb689c6394d4e57ee2f16559ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-139033/selection/0da528af2c2acad7fec81ac76c9e2234623a78033071f03cfd0f0ba2dfe62328" "q-139033/selection/f324691e69eb403cb0b9bdd1ac58cbe4b22fc42bac4b770eff8a1ad615df91f2" "q-139033/selection/6d7038b0195af33f0b9d34b41262abc27fdb6a77628d13b17b3a8eee950a47b9" "q-139033/selection/bbbbd34f56a2efc420f6cbfa1a9dec7d83a77c89f28fb2022e019d544a5793ce" "q-139033/selection/d388a7a600d680356d80ea3c3d43fb27b9a478fb689c6394d4e57ee2f16559ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139033" :question/id #uuid "1b26a4f7-3d3e-5904-9022-e3e7b7a8173a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139033" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{2}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139033/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139033"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139058/selection/d40fb7c8f53c01b1436c321985dd80bc2502d3a1973c0c44d77f30de0fe51944" :answer/id #uuid "2dc39450-12e2-564c-a36d-93dac60224ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139058/selection/cac945913e1c0629afa5725450c58cea7d5c7d00a8bfc2e398cdfd4b93e641bc" :answer/id #uuid "324c7098-fcc8-53cc-8363-2667fd6628d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139058/selection/bca9521d82d916723500c6917340e1020f7b229d6b50f26fcaebf0261d360fe7" :answer/id #uuid "00a18554-0191-593c-a54c-4bebf5d0f427" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{2t}\\sqrt{{3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139058/selection/65bbba1c8b6b666f295afc2f602e9176f22c166c3bb3b38affc07124329810e1" :answer/id #uuid "242732c0-b774-5611-aa78-4c6e9f5f6802" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139058/selection/3cd23e2791d9ad6bf2f57d9773f3d60f5bef7f951131227d2fbbd7c5547a2fd2" :answer/id #uuid "b6748ced-2f74-58fe-88ca-e944242c6b0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{2t}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139058/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "127a0402-3270-542e-b435-383b46e8565d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139058/selection/d40fb7c8f53c01b1436c321985dd80bc2502d3a1973c0c44d77f30de0fe51944" :answer-field/choices ["q-139058/selection/d40fb7c8f53c01b1436c321985dd80bc2502d3a1973c0c44d77f30de0fe51944" "q-139058/selection/cac945913e1c0629afa5725450c58cea7d5c7d00a8bfc2e398cdfd4b93e641bc" "q-139058/selection/bca9521d82d916723500c6917340e1020f7b229d6b50f26fcaebf0261d360fe7" "q-139058/selection/65bbba1c8b6b666f295afc2f602e9176f22c166c3bb3b38affc07124329810e1" "q-139058/selection/3cd23e2791d9ad6bf2f57d9773f3d60f5bef7f951131227d2fbbd7c5547a2fd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139058" :question/id #uuid "a6e8f662-ee40-5cf7-8a01-8ad4b064cc0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139058" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={3}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({3}^{t})={3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{({3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139058/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139058"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352089/field-1/73d7b4cc74aa446fe7ccbd3b6cc2cdf2c7004d555f3df76704134be16b913b35" :answer/id #uuid "fa374d7c-4411-5a62-8563-5e84feaa60c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{160\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352089/field-1/86dab25cc9031ec163e2f159de46a569ba421c7304483fab7c9be8c6b12a747e" :answer/id #uuid "c60d6a6f-1f82-5747-8247-1b9fffe2c1cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352089/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e43e6c-e7b0-5460-bf0b-c8d4a4c46570" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352089/field-1/73d7b4cc74aa446fe7ccbd3b6cc2cdf2c7004d555f3df76704134be16b913b35" :answer-field/choices ["q-352089/field-1/73d7b4cc74aa446fe7ccbd3b6cc2cdf2c7004d555f3df76704134be16b913b35" "q-352089/field-1/86dab25cc9031ec163e2f159de46a569ba421c7304483fab7c9be8c6b12a747e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352089" :question/id #uuid "f78d6a56-cd43-5d8a-9efe-3b87eb7543f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352089" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}-t,\\,y=4{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}-t)={e}^{t}-1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{t/2})=2{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t}-1)}^{2}+{(2{e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{t}+1+4{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}8\\pi {e}^{t/2}({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u={e}^{t/2}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t,$ or $2\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}8\\pi ({e}^{t}+1)\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}8\\pi ({u}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi ((\\frac{8}{3}+2)-(\\frac{1}{3}+1)) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{160\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-352089/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352089"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-138979/selection/30475f43b6c4cf25decd77bd7cf2fb9fa1dad498f4d6eb8070a20c73efba98cc" :answer/id #uuid "126ed4ce-05f5-5602-b5ae-359f8c05eea8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2{e}^{2}-e+3)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138979/selection/345c89d18411a1c63dc2a4ff3d39352da7fee959eb6fc2b1e7abc6ec3a124155" :answer/id #uuid "93ebf8c2-3d9a-5574-a106-c64d37fbea9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{2}+6e)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138979/selection/cc9fa2308d3749ac3012d572f474d7436d088d298e728acd73ee13df779a5a76" :answer/id #uuid "92643f81-b5b9-5353-912b-bc8dfeb53172" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{2}+3e-1)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138979/selection/141d2fdbc0ea1f617cb325d41726ed229a5a55d52516c4068fc2309333ba78eb" :answer/id #uuid "54acce81-94f4-54f0-a692-de9afe9ae4e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{2}-3)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138979/selection/9b03d4998adfbca3aba9bde5a770ecb2fc0774d319f8ffd2f9d0d9650b770b5f" :answer/id #uuid "5528fe93-264c-5cc9-88c4-d7f1db75e367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{2}+2e-6)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-138979/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd13f50-13be-5f7e-81d7-9175e4bc527f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-138979/selection/9b03d4998adfbca3aba9bde5a770ecb2fc0774d319f8ffd2f9d0d9650b770b5f" :answer-field/choices ["q-138979/selection/30475f43b6c4cf25decd77bd7cf2fb9fa1dad498f4d6eb8070a20c73efba98cc" "q-138979/selection/345c89d18411a1c63dc2a4ff3d39352da7fee959eb6fc2b1e7abc6ec3a124155" "q-138979/selection/cc9fa2308d3749ac3012d572f474d7436d088d298e728acd73ee13df779a5a76" "q-138979/selection/141d2fdbc0ea1f617cb325d41726ed229a5a55d52516c4068fc2309333ba78eb" "q-138979/selection/9b03d4998adfbca3aba9bde5a770ecb2fc0774d319f8ffd2f9d0d9650b770b5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-138979" :question/id #uuid "7666324e-f52a-5926-a931-a51fd1f8d7b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138979" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}-t,\\,y=4{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}-t)={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{t/2})=2{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{({e}^{t}-1)}^{2}+{(2{e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{t}+1+4{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression ${e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot |{e}^{t}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot ({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ using integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⇒ & v={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x & =t{e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi [{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t] \\\\  & =2\\pi [{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{1}-{(t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t})|}_{0}^{1}] \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}+1-0)-(e-e)+(0-1)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+e-3] \\\\  & =({e}^{2}+2e-6)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-138979/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-138979"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139013/selection/a0bad32271d0cd121944e11b49c9ac1990742a44eb2a55f3cdc3c7d786a6d9e9" :answer/id #uuid "27f5d1e5-8f0d-5b3f-b8fb-6c49a479c445" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{10}\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139013/selection/8618ebabfe4c90dc3828093071aed25aee2c9b121c970da90674e33c855e7fb8" :answer/id #uuid "ed493aeb-a556-5255-b78e-69bf1c0e2e6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{696\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139013/selection/f4d44b78da5f8922a064c75d46e5bbf0062c5740a0ff123274b433018665ebf6" :answer/id #uuid "85c068ff-af87-5898-a9a7-b875c727d28e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{142\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139013/selection/44db06dbc68a95cc6ce0ec46ab3e37c25aa80b1f5bf0449ca68aeeabab04d30d" :answer/id #uuid "46593d64-766b-5bc0-b7c7-ee94624ecae0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{112\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139013/selection/7d53524f93699f173a237fb02d581311aa90235fa6802e3c6909b8d8dc56abf6" :answer/id #uuid "d5973c4d-0eff-5335-bbf6-d583703c6248" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{112\\sqrt{6}\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139013/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f642a3ca-6d4a-52c8-94d2-72a42bae25d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139013/selection/8618ebabfe4c90dc3828093071aed25aee2c9b121c970da90674e33c855e7fb8" :answer-field/choices ["q-139013/selection/a0bad32271d0cd121944e11b49c9ac1990742a44eb2a55f3cdc3c7d786a6d9e9" "q-139013/selection/8618ebabfe4c90dc3828093071aed25aee2c9b121c970da90674e33c855e7fb8" "q-139013/selection/f4d44b78da5f8922a064c75d46e5bbf0062c5740a0ff123274b433018665ebf6" "q-139013/selection/44db06dbc68a95cc6ce0ec46ab3e37c25aa80b1f5bf0449ca68aeeabab04d30d" "q-139013/selection/7d53524f93699f173a237fb02d581311aa90235fa6802e3c6909b8d8dc56abf6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139013" :question/id #uuid "991b574c-848e-52ed-a23f-5d0fcf3bface" :question/math-academy-id "q-139013" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6t)}^{2}+{(6{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t] \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\cdot |6t|\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\cdot 6t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{2}$ Then, ${t}^{2}=u-1$ and $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =18\\pi [(\\frac{2({4}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{2({4}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})] \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot [(\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3})-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot (\\frac{62}{5}-\\frac{14}{3}) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot \\frac{116}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{696\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-139013/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139013"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-348298/field-1/f7fdc64fbc107742a601cee3b05bcae40a00aedcfa30df56d25ae29c28c2601c" :answer/id #uuid "089067a8-8bca-5f3c-9018-0d49018d4701" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{192\\pi}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348298/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "579db1e0-fab8-5f42-bd70-7d25ebdc19a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-348298/field-1/f7fdc64fbc107742a601cee3b05bcae40a00aedcfa30df56d25ae29c28c2601c" :answer-field/choices ["q-348298/field-1/f7fdc64fbc107742a601cee3b05bcae40a00aedcfa30df56d25ae29c28c2601c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-348298" :question/id #uuid "bc71c7c0-487c-5e77-be2e-dfb6e6268d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-348298" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6t-2{t}^{3},\\,y=6{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2{t}^{3})=6-6{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6-6{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(12t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36-72{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+144{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot 6\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =72\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{192\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-348298/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-348298"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139025/selection/5426de72636d910500a6032234bad0c56db2a10fd82995247f0bdb284f5193f0" :answer/id #uuid "7862822a-e03e-5f40-83f3-7aef7946f34a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{{e}^{2}+2}{{e}^{2}})\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139025/selection/bdc75d273f9f806028a30d3c2ee524ded48808223f4cb3541b286b7b21e89893" :answer/id #uuid "9198514d-d36a-5595-9939-5135ccefb075" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{{e}^{2}}+1)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139025/selection/d8ed20f489757afc8f781c311e499013494362fc31d106eca69f33bc544c81e1" :answer/id #uuid "e41555fb-38ff-5edf-8910-19d886355697" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{{e}^{4}+1}{{e}^{2}}+2)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139025/selection/ec4f224bfbd85bc90a4bc5c24b620c5f62525659537bcffb4f622cb089667c77" :answer/id #uuid "443ad3a0-2a02-5895-b24b-5eb8e36f2f31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}}+4)\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139025/selection/f62b98eaa7999eb0bf694f82de65fb03b4e7676f7bf22d4ecaf4907c67a373f2" :answer/id #uuid "bdabb38f-f0c7-5d34-87cb-116351979088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}})\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139025/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17f20ae3-ebff-5833-adf8-213d9c65004d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139025/selection/ec4f224bfbd85bc90a4bc5c24b620c5f62525659537bcffb4f622cb089667c77" :answer-field/choices ["q-139025/selection/5426de72636d910500a6032234bad0c56db2a10fd82995247f0bdb284f5193f0" "q-139025/selection/bdc75d273f9f806028a30d3c2ee524ded48808223f4cb3541b286b7b21e89893" "q-139025/selection/d8ed20f489757afc8f781c311e499013494362fc31d106eca69f33bc544c81e1" "q-139025/selection/ec4f224bfbd85bc90a4bc5c24b620c5f62525659537bcffb4f622cb089667c77" "q-139025/selection/f62b98eaa7999eb0bf694f82de65fb03b4e7676f7bf22d4ecaf4907c67a373f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139025" :question/id #uuid "416dd5c5-ff78-5ba5-bd10-1893f9bb5102" :question/math-academy-id "q-139025" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=1$ to ${t}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{1}{t})=1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{{(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot |1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+t+\\frac{2}{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+t+\\frac{2}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+2)-(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+0)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2{e}^{2}}+2] \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}}+4)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-139025/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139025"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9866"] :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9867"] :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9869"] :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9(1+t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u = 1+t.$ Then, $1+3t=3u-2$ and $\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{6{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{4{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{6({2}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{4({2}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}(16\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
